Expressing the elements 
\begin{slide}



- Explicit: listing all elements. 

\[A = \{1,2,4\} , B = \{1,3,5,7,\ldots\}\] 

- Implicit: stating conditions. 

\[A = \{ animals | has four legs \} , B = \{x \, | \, x is an even number \}\]

\end{slide}




Size 
\begin{slide}


- Finite set: the number of elements is an integer. 

\[ A = \{ atoms in the universe \} , B = \{ money you have \}\]

- Infinite set: 

\[ A = \{ integers \} , B = \{ twin primes \} \]

\end{slide}




Continuum
\begin{slide}


- Discrete: elements are ``apart'' from each other. 

\[ A = \{ 1,3,4,5\} \] 

- Continuous: elements are in a range. 

\[ A = \{ real numbers \} , B = \{ x\, | \, 0 < x \leq 10 \} \]

\end{slide}




There is a commonly used notation of sets called \red{interval}. This express the sets expressed by inequality signs.
{}
\begin{eqnarray*}
[a,b] &=& \{ x \, | \, a \leq x \leq b \} 

(a,b) &=& \{ x \, | \, a < x < b \} 

(a,b] &=& \{ x \, | \, a < x \leq b \} 

[ a,b ) &=& \{ x \, | \, a \leq x < b \}
\end{eqnarray*}



- ``$($'' and ``$)$'' means non-inclusive or open. This corresponds to $<$ or $>$ signs.

- ``$[$'' and ``$]$'' means inclusive or closed. This corresponds to $\leq$ or $\geq$ signs.






